Put the current-context thread_locals behind a macro

OpenAL Soft keeps two `thread_local`s in `ALCcontext`: the context a thread
made current with `alcSetThreadContext`, and a guard object whose destructor
releases it when the thread ends. They back one extension,
ALC_EXT_thread_local_context, and `GetContextRef` reads the first on every AL
call.

A `thread_local` here compiles to `__tls_get_addr` calls and, for the guard,
`__cxa_thread_atexit`. libkernel exports the first, but whether a payload's
module carries a TLS segment the loader sets up has never been measured - and
if it does not, every AL call faults, not just the extension's.

So both become `ALSOFT_THREAD_LOCAL`, which is `thread_local` unless the build
says otherwise, and `oops-openal-soft.mk` defines it empty. Each is then one
process-wide variable: `alcSetThreadContext` sets a context for every thread
rather than the caller's, and `alcMakeContextCurrent` - what SuperTux and
SuperTuxKart call - is unaffected, because it was always process-wide.

Drop this patch, and the define, once thread_local is measured on hardware.

